The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (dma_mapping_error(priv->device, dma_addr)) {
    netdev_err(priv->dev, "%s: DMA mapping error\n", __func__);
    ret = NETDEV_TX_OK;
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
When `dma_map_single()` fails, the code sets `ret = NETDEV_TX_OK` and jumps to `out` without freeing `skb`. Returning `NETDEV_TX_OK` tells the network stack that the packet has been fully consumed, so the stack does not free the skb, causing a memory leak on every DMA mapping failure.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
if (dma_mapping_error(priv->device, dma_addr)) {
    netdev_err(priv->dev, "%s: DMA mapping error\n", __func__);
    dev_kfree_skb_any(skb);
    ret = NETDEV_TX_OK;
    goto out;
}
```
Reason:
Adding `dev_kfree_skb_any(skb)` before the jump ensures the skb is properly freed when a DMA mapping error occurs, preventing the memory leak while still returning `NETDEV_TX_OK` (since the packet is not transmitted).

